  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nBehind the low today, expect a lot less 'interesting' weather \nconditions as the ingredients conducive for severe weather continue \nto retreat southeastward away from the area. That being said, with a \nbackdoor cold front moving through today across the U.P., expect \ngusty northwest winds of 25 to 30 mph to pick up across the area by \nthis afternoon, with some spots in the Keweenaw and east along the \nLake Superior shoreline potentially seeing some gusts up to 40 mph; \na rogue gust or two up to 45 mph or so cannot be completely ruled \nout (up to a 30% chance according to the latest 00z HREF). Because \nof the gusty winds over Lake Superior, dangerous rip currents are \nexpected to develop along the shoreline. Therefore, a Beach Hazard \nStatement has been issued for the beaches of Marquette and Alger \ncounties today. \n\nWhile the severe weather threat is now over, due to some synoptic \nmoisture still hanging over us in the lower to mid levels (as can be \nseen on 850 and 700mb moisture content and transfer plots), delta-Ts \nover Lake Superior getting to around 8-10 C, and strong winds over \nthe lake creating additional moisture flux (as well as help from \nupslope lift), some lake enhanced-like light rain showers (and a \nweak thunderstorm or two) are expected to form over much of the area \ntoday, mainly during the morning hours as the backdoor cold front is \npassing through. However, once the additional system moisture is \nlost behind the cold front, expect the rain chances to drop down to \naround 25%-ish or less by this afternoon. While there is around a \n20% chance for some light rain showers over the east late this \nevening, expect the last of the rain showers to be done over us by \nmidnight tonight. \n\nBesides the rain and gusty winds today, expect the rest of this work \nweek to generally be calmer, with highs today and Wednesday looking \nto be below normal (mid 60s to maybe lower 70s by this morning \ntoday, and the lower to mid 70s on Wednesday). In addition, with \nrainfall having occurred over the wildfires in northern Ontario and \nthe Boundary Waters of Minnesota, as well as high pressure ridging \ndropping down from the Canadian Prairies into the Midwest, wildfire \nsmoke is suspected to finally not impact the air quality! However, \nas the high pressure flattens and progresses east through the Lower \nGreat Lakes into New England for Thursday into Friday, expect the \ntemperatures to begin climbing back up to above normal (think high \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to mid 80s). As this occurs, troughing \nslowly moves in from the west towards us, with a shortwave low \npotentially bringing some more rain showers and thunderstorms over \nus by this weekend. Behind this low, upper-level northwest flow \nlooks to keep temperatures somewhat moderated over us into early \nnext week.",
